%% The Topic 5 revision sheet — included by KB Part Five and by the RN volume. Must make sense on its own.

## 1. What "status" means and the framework

- **Status of women** — women's position in society: **rights, resources, respect and power** compared with men's. Four syllabus dimensions: **health, education, employment, law**; plus politics, intersectionality, digital access, climate.
- **Constitution 1973** — **Art. 25** (equality; no discrimination on the basis of sex; special provision for women allowed); **25A** (free, compulsory education 5–16, 18th Amendment 2010); **26–27** (no sex discrimination in public places or service); **32** (women in local government); **34** (full participation of women); **35** (protect marriage, family, mother, child); **37(e)** (maternity benefits); **51, 59, 106** (reserved seats — 60 in the NA). **Fundamental rights** (Arts 8–28) are enforceable in court; **Principles of Policy** (29–40) are goals.
- **Headline figures** — MMR **186** (2019); female literacy **~53%** (men ~68%; Census 2023); female LFPR **24.4%** (2024–25); GGGR 2026 **143/145**.

## 2. Measuring status — the Global Gender Gap (2018, 2026)

- **Global Gender Gap Index** — World Economic Forum, since **2006**; how much of the **gap** between women and men is closed (0–100%) in four sub-indices: **economic participation and opportunity** (labour force, wages, income, senior roles), **educational attainment** (literacy, enrolment), **health and survival** (sex ratio at birth, healthy life expectancy), **political empowerment** (parliament, ministers, heads of state). Measures **gaps**, not well-being.
- **Other measures** — UNDP **GII** (2010), **GDI** (1995); World Bank *Women, Business and the Law*; SDG 5 indicators.
- **Pakistan's record** — 2006 **112/115**; 2017 **143/144** ("second-worst" — 2018 question); 2021 153/156; 2023 **142/146** (MCQ 2024); 2025 **148/148**, **56.7%**, zero women ministers; 2026 **143/145**, **59.5%** (strongest gain in a decade), sub-ranks **economic 144th, education 130th, political 107th**; lowest in South Asia and of 38 lower-middle-income economies. Global gap **69.2%**, parity **120 years**, Iceland first. Distinguish **rank** from **score**.
- **Why so low (four sub-indices)** — economic: LFPR 24.4% vs roughly 80% for men; one of the widest pay gaps (ILO); few women managers; informal/unpaid work. Education: literacy 53/68; 25m+ out of school, mostly girls; rural, Balochistan and KP gaps. Health: sex ratio, MMR 186, anaemia. Politics: 60 reserved seats but only **11** women won general seats (2024); no women ministers (2025).
- **Why commitments (Constitution, CEDAW 1996, Beijing 1995, SDGs 2015) have not closed the gap** — enforcement gap; patriarchal norms (purdah, honour, son preference, early marriage); poverty and low spending (health ~1%, education ~2% of GDP); unsafe transport and harassment; unpaid care, no childcare; weak political will and voice; crises (militancy, 2022 floods, economic crises); uneven provincial capacity after the **18th Amendment (2010)**.
- **South Asia** — Bangladesh (garment jobs, women leaders), Sri Lanka (literacy, maternal health), Nepal (2015 quotas) show policy, not religion or region, decides.
- **Moving up** — women's paid work (transport, childcare, harassment law, protected home/digital work); pay gap; senior posts; girls' secondary schooling; maternal health; appoint women ministers.
- **Critique of the index** — consistent and comparable vs measures gaps not well-being, misses violence, unpaid care and informal work, data dated, rank swings. Signal, not whole story.

## 3. Women's health (2017, 2023)

- **Definition** — WHO (1946): complete physical, mental and social **well-being**. Life cycle: before birth/infancy (son preference) → childhood (malnutrition) → adolescence (early marriage, anaemia) → reproductive years → older age (NCDs, cancers).
- **Indicators** — MMR **186** (PMMS 2019); anaemia ~**42%** of women 15–49 (NNS 2018); stunting ~**40%** of under-fives (NNS 2018); TFR ~**3.6**; contraceptive use ~**34%**; skilled birth attendance ~**two-thirds** (PDHS 2017–18); health spending ~**1% of GDP**.
- **Problems** — maternal death; malnutrition and anaemia (women eat last; intergenerational cycle); high fertility; early marriage; RTIs and fistula; **breast cancer** (MCQ 2019: ~13,000 deaths a year; late diagnosis; October awareness month) and cervical cancer (HPV vaccine introduced **2025**); NCDs; **mental health** (depression and anxiety from violence, isolation, forced marriage; few psychiatrists; stigma); violence; disasters (650,000 pregnant women in 2022 flood areas).
- **Causes** — social/cultural (son preference, early marriage, restricted mobility, low decision power, preference for female doctors); economic (poverty, food insecurity, costs — mostly **out-of-pocket**); health system (low spending, few female staff, rural gaps, women graduates not practising); education (low literacy, taboos).
- **Programmes** — **LHW Programme 1994** (Benazir; ~1,000 people per LHW; family planning, antenatal care, immunisation; SC regularisation **2012**; jobs for rural women); community midwives; **Sehat Sahulat** health card (2015–16; hospital care, not primary care); BISP and Benazir Nashonuma; Arts 35 and 37(e); health provincial after 2010.
- **Improve within available resources (2017)** — primary care first; LHWs and midwives; family planning; iron, folic acid, fortification; skilled birth attendance and referral; female workforce and telemedicine; cancer screening awareness; engage men and mothers-in-law; enforce child-marriage laws; target with BISP data; mental health in primary care.
- **Status "as a whole" (2023)** — link health to education, work, law and political voice.

## 4. Maternal health and Fathalla (2024)

- **Mahmoud Fathalla** — Egyptian obstetrician (later FIGO president); **Safe Motherhood Conference, Nairobi 1987** (launched the **Safe Motherhood Initiative**); "**Why did Mrs X die?**"; maternal death = end of a long **road** built by society; most deaths preventable; "women are not dying because of diseases we cannot treat… but because societies have yet to decide their lives are worth saving".
- **The road** — born female and poor → undernourished and anaemic → little schooling → married young → no family planning, many close pregnancies → no antenatal care → complication (haemorrhage, eclampsia, obstructed labour, infection, unsafe abortion) → delays → death.
- **Three delays** (**Thaddeus and Maine 1994**) — **deciding** (danger not recognised; permission; cost), **reaching** (distance, roads, transport, floods), **receiving** care (no staff, blood, drugs, theatre).
- **Data** — MMR 186 (worse in rural areas, Balochistan); skilled attendance ~two-thirds; anaemia ~42%; TFR 3.6; contraception 34%, unmet need ~1 in 6; **SDG 3.1** MMR < 70 by 2030.
- **Social context** — son preference; literacy 53%; poverty; low decision power.
- **Cultural context** — early marriage (girls' federal age **16**; 18 only in **Sindh 2013**, **Islamabad 2025**; Punjab bill **2026**); purdah and escorts; female-doctor preference; home births with *dais*; taboos; pressure for sons.
- **Demographic context** — **241 million** (Census 2023), growing **2.55%**; young; rural majority; regional inequality; floods.
- **Closing the road** — delay marriage, keep girls in school; nutrition; family planning (LHWs, pharmacies, scholars); antenatal care (WHO: 8 contacts); skilled attendance; emergency obstetric care with blood banks, ambulances (Rescue 1122); female workforce; community awareness; maternal death reviews.
- **Pakistani "Mrs X"** — Balochistan girl: fed last, left school (distant school), married at 15, six births, anaemia, bleeding at home, waited for husband, four hours to blood — every stage a policy failure.

## 5. Women's education (never asked — blind spot)

- **Why it matters** — later marriage; fewer, healthier children; antenatal care and immunisation; higher earnings; voice; knowledge of rights; educated daughters (virtuous cycle).
- **Article 25A** (2010) — free, compulsory education 5–16; provincial laws; weak enforcement.
- **Status** — literacy **60.65%** (men ~68%, women ~53%; Census 2023); 2019 paper **48%** (MCQ); WEF female literacy ~48.5% (2025); **25m+** out of school, mostly girls; education rank **130th** (2026); dropout after primary; women ~half of university students, majority in medicine; low learning; missing facilities.
- **Provinces** — Punjab and Islamabad lead; rural Sindh lags; KP (former tribal areas) improving after conflict; **Balochistan** lowest.
- **Causes** — supply (few girls' middle/secondary schools, distance, no female teachers, no toilets or walls); demand (poverty, costs, girls' housework, early marriage, "belongs to another household"); safety and conflict (harassment; **Swat** ban 2007–09; **Malala** shot **9 Oct 2012**, BBC diary 2009, UN speech 12 July 2013, Nobel **2014**); floods 2022.
- **Other issues** — "**doctor brides**" (graduates not practising; telemedicine as a fix); madrassas (free, near, accepted; limited modern subjects; mainstreaming); adult literacy and non-formal education.
- **Programmes** — Punjab girls' stipend (2004); BISP Waseela-e-Taleem; community/home schools; female teachers; missing-facilities programmes; non-formal education; scholarships.
- **Way forward** — enforce 25A; nearby secondary schools or safe transport; local female teachers; stipends; 18-year marriage age; protect and rebuild schools; change norms; link education to jobs and digital skills; raise spending above ~2% of GDP.

## 6. Women and employment (2019)

- **Data** — female LFPR **24.4%** (LFS 2024–25; 21.4% in 2020–21); Punjab **33.8%**, KP **21.1%**, Sindh **20.5%**, Balochistan **14.5%**; mostly agriculture and informal; economic rank **144th**.
- **Where** — agriculture (unpaid family work; cotton picking; livestock); home-based piece work via middlemen; domestic work; teaching, nursing, LHWs; manufacturing; professions and IT (small, growing).
- **Reproductive roles and double burden** — bearing and raising children, household maintenance; Moser's **triple role** (productive, reproductive, community); **second shift** (Hochschild 1989); women do ~**3×** men's unpaid care (ILO 2018).
- **Worldwide challenges** — double burden; pay gap; **comparable worth** (equal pay for work of equal value; MCQ 2023); occupational segregation; **glass ceiling** (invisible barriers to the top; MCQ 2024); **sticky floor** (low-wage, low-mobility jobs; MCQ 2026); motherhood penalty; harassment; lack of childcare and maternity protection; informality; social disapproval.
- **Pakistan's challenges** — mobility and safety (transport, street harassment; women-only buses); norms (breadwinner, honour; teaching/medicine accepted); joint-family double burden; informality and wide pay gap; **Harassment Act 2010** (amended **2022**; committees, ombudsperson; weak in private/informal sectors); childcare scarce; **Maternity and Paternity Leave Act 2020** (federal), **Sindh Maternity Benefits Act 2018**; missing educated women; **10% federal quota** (MCQ key: PML-Q, 2002), unfilled posts.
- **Agriculture detail** — unpaid; wages to husbands; no land, credit, extension; heat and pesticides.
- **Home-based detail** — piece rates, middlemen, no protection; **Sindh HBW Act 2018** (minimum wage, social security); Home-Based Women Workers Federation; **Punjab Domestic Workers Act 2019**.
- **Formal sector** — banks, civil service, army, police, courts; **Justice Ayesha Malik**, first woman SC judge (**2022**); few senior women.
- **Benefits of work** — income, independence, networks, voice (Kabeer 2000 on Bangladesh).
- **Way forward** — safe transport and harassment enforcement; childcare; paternity leave and shared care; equal pay; protect informal workers; skills and digital work; women in management; count unpaid care (SDG 5.4).

## 7. Constitution and family law

- **Rights table** — Art. 8 (void inconsistent laws); 9, 14 (security; dignity); 11 (no slavery, trafficking); 25, 25A, 26, 27, 32, 34, 35, 37(e), 51/59/106.
- **Family-law sources** — Shariat Act 1937 (and West Punjab 1948 — agricultural land); **Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act 1939**; **MFLO 1961**; **Family Courts Act 1964**; Child Marriage Restraint Act 1929; Guardians and Wards Act 1890; **Dowry and Bridal Gifts (Restriction) Act 1976**; **Sindh Hindu Marriage Act 2016**, **Hindu Marriage Act 2017**.
- **MFLO 1961** (Ayub; after Bogra's 1955 marriage and the Rashid Commission) — marriage **registration**; polygamy needs **Arbitration Council** permission; *talaq* notice, effective after **90 days**; maintenance; **orphaned grandchildren** inherit; girls' marriage age raised to **16**.
- **Divorce and dower** — judicial divorce (1939 grounds); ***khula*** (***Khurshid Bibi*** **1967**); family-court amendments 2002, 2015; ***haq mehr***; maintenance; *talaq-e-tafweez* in the *nikah-nama*.
- **Child marriage** — federal **16**; **Sindh 2013 = 18**; **ICT 2025 = 18** (assent 30 May 2025; CII opposed); Punjab bill **April 2026**; **FSC 2021**: minimum age not against Islam.
- **Inheritance** — Islamic shares (daughter half a son's); denied by custom (*haq bakhshwana*); **Anti-Women Practices Act 2011**; **Enforcement of Women's Property Rights Act 2020** (ombudsperson); very low female land ownership.
- **Custody** — Guardians and Wards Act 1890; child's welfare; mothers' *hizanat* of young children.
- **Cases** — *Khurshid Bibi* 1967 (*khula*); **Saima Waheed** (LHC 1997, **SC 2004**: adult woman may marry without wali); FSC 2021; inheritance cannot be taken by custom.
- **Dowry** — burden; 1976 Act unenforced; violence over dowry; mehr is the husband's duty.
- **Assessment** — gains (registration, notice, polygamy limits, *khula*, maintenance, family courts, marriage ages) vs gaps (rural registration, unpermitted polygamy, age 16 remains, inheritance denied, slow courts, costs, low awareness).

## 8. Protective laws, human rights, enforcement (2020)

- **Hudood (1979) → 2006** — Zina Ordinance endangered rape complainants; **Protection of Women (Criminal Laws Amendment) Act 2006** (Musharraf; MCQ 2016) moved rape to the PPC.
- **Protective laws** — Criminal Law (Amendment) Act **2004** (honour); **2006**; **Harassment Act 2010** (amended 2022); s. 509 PPC 2010 (public harassment); **Acid Act 2011**; **Anti-Women Practices Act 2011** (forced marriage, *vani/swara*, marriage to the Qur'an, inheritance); DV laws: **Sindh 2013**, **Balochistan 2014**, **Punjab 2016**, **KP 2021**, **Islamabad 2025**; **honour law Oct 2016** (mandatory life; after **Qandeel Baloch**, July 2016); **anti-rape amendment 2016** (DNA); **PECA 2016**; **Transgender Act 2018** (FSC 2023; appeal pending); **Property Rights Act 2020**; **Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act 2021** (special courts, investigation units, crisis cells); **ICT child-marriage law 2025**.
- **Institutions** — **NCSW 2000** (Musharraf; MCQ 2016; NCSW Act 2012); **National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women 2002** (MCQ 2018); provincial commissions; **NCHR (Act 2012)**; Ministry of Human Rights (2015); harassment ombudspersons; Punjab Women Protection Authority, **VAW centre Multan 2017**; women's police stations (1994); family courts (1964); shelters (***Dar-ul-Aman***); helpline **1043**; Rescue 1122.
- **International** — CEDAW 1996; CRC 1990; ICESCR 2008; ICCPR 2010; CAT 2010; UPR; **GSP+ 2014** (27 conventions).
- **2020 statement** — three requirements: **guarantee** rights (laws, institutions — largely present); **environment for enjoyment** (awareness, access, acceptance — weak); **operative safeguards and speedy remedy** (police, courts, commissions — weakest). Wording echoes the **NCHR Act 2012** preamble. Pyramid: guarantee → enjoyment → remedy.
- **Honour killing case** — Qisas and Diyat (1990/1997) "forgiveness" loophole; Qandeel Baloch; Oct 2016 law; still hundreds a year; disguised or settled.
- **Enforcement gap causes** — low awareness; police attitudes, few women police; slow, costly courts, weak forensics, low convictions; family pressure, *jirgas*; weak, under-funded commissions and shelters; uneven laws after 2010; CII opposition; norms over law.
- **Closing the gap** — legal literacy; gender-sensitive policing; special courts, legal aid, forensics; one-stop centres; strong commissions; harmonised laws (18 everywhere); engage scholars; publish data.

## 9. Intersectional disadvantage (2026)

- **Intersectionality** — **Crenshaw 1989**: overlapping identities multiply disadvantage; "which women?"; **Collins 1990**: matrix of domination (laws, institutions, practices, ideas).
- **Rural women** — clinics far, few female doctors, home births; low literacy, few secondary schools; unpaid farm work, no land; feudal/tribal power, *jirgas*, *vani/swara*, honour violence.
- **Religious minorities** — reported **forced conversion and marriage** of Hindu and Christian girls (Sindh, Punjab); scheduled-caste Hindu women in bonded labour (Thar, Sindh); Christian women in sanitation and domestic work; biased textbooks; Hindu marriages unregistrable until **2016/2017**; Ahmadi women.
- **Ethnic minorities** — Hazara widows (Quetta); Baloch women with worst indicators and conflict; former tribal areas (merged with KP **2018**) — militancy, schools destroyed; Afghan refugee women.
- **Disabled women** — stigma, school exclusion, inaccessible transport and clinics, reproductive neglect, poor marriage prospects, higher abuse; **1981** job quota ordinance; ICT disability law 2020; **CRPD ratified 2011**; weak data.
- **Others** — home-based and domestic workers; widows and women-headed households; older women (no pensions, IDs); ***khawaja siras***.
- **Two lives** — Lahore banker vs Tharparkar Hindu labourer.
- **Policy** — averages hide the worst-off; single-axis policies miss people; design for overlaps (mobile clinics, accessible girls' schools, protection from forced conversion, social security, CNIC drives); disaggregated data; voice in commissions.

## 10. The digital gender gap (2026 note)

- **Definition** — gap in **access** (phone, internet), **skills/use**, **benefits**. Measured by **GSMA** (*Mobile Gender Gap Report*); South Asia has the widest gaps.
- **Progress** — mobile-internet gap **38% → 25%**; **~8 million** more women online (2024–25). Drivers: cheaper phones and data; digital BISP payments; skills programmes; social media and freelancing; more women's CNICs.
- **Barriers** — cost; **CNIC** needed for a SIM; literacy (~53%); norms (disapproval; shared or male-controlled phones); online harassment, blackmail, image abuse; lack of relevant local-language services. Gap widest for rural, older, poorer, less-educated women.
- **Implications** — opportunities: home-based income (freelancing, e-commerce), mobile banking, telemedicine, online learning, voice and activism. Risks: harassment, family surveillance, insecure platform work, scams, a new divide.
- **Law and help** — **PECA 2016** (amended 2025); FIA cybercrime wing (moving to a new agency); **Digital Rights Foundation** helpline (2016); most women don't report.
- **Way forward** — affordable access and CNIC drives; digital skills via schools, LHWs, BISP; safety by design and women-friendly reporting; women-centred services in local languages; protect freelancers; data; **SDG 5.b**.

## 11. Politics and climate

- **Politics** — NA **336** (266 general, **60** women, 10 non-Muslim); Senate **17** women's seats (2002); **2024**: 306 women contested general seats, **11** won; **zero** women ministers (2025); **Maryam Nawaz** first woman CM (Feb 2024); **Benazir Bhutto** PM (1988); **Fahmida Mirza** Speaker (2008); **Elections Act 2017** (5% tickets; void if women's turnout < 10%); millions fewer women voters (CNIC gap); **33%** local seats under devolution (2000–01), later reduced; barriers: family and tribal opposition, cost, patronage, harassment, vote bans; **Women's Parliamentary Caucus 2008** drove 2010–2016 laws; women in bureaucracy (10% quota) and judiciary.
- **Climate** — Pakistan **< 1%** of emissions; 2022 floods (650,000 pregnant women; schools damaged; livestock lost; relief to male heads, CNIC barriers; early-marriage reports); heat waves (**Jacobabad**; kitchens, fields, pregnancy, water, power cuts, home-based income).
- **Linking the dimensions (2023)** — education → health and earnings; health → work; work → voice; law → protection; politics → policy. Key programmes: LHWs 1994; Art. 25A; Harassment Act 2010; MFLO 1961; 2006 Act; 60 seats; PECA 2016; BISP 2008.

## 12. Debates in one line each

- **2026 rise** — real but economic gap unchanged (144th; 24.4%).
- **Low female LFPR** — norms, safety, care, jobs, measurement; fastest levers: transport, childcare, harassment enforcement, protected home/digital work.
- **Child marriage 16 vs 18** — health, education, SDG 5.3, FSC 2021 vs CII view; enforce and register.
- **Laws vs enforcement** — enforcement first.
- **Inheritance** — remedies (2011, 2020) plus automatic mutation, scholars, support.
- **Digital** — access plus skills and safety.
- **Health workforce** — LHWs, midwives, telemedicine, rural incentives.
- **Leave no woman behind** — disaggregated data; design for overlaps.
